 Record design decisions, hypotheses, review findings, and unresolved questions here.
 Imported associated notes retain original evidence/timestamps in `evidence/associated_notes.jsonl`.
+
+## 2026-10-24 learnability repair
+
+A source-bound seed-8 lesson showed that the plausible rule “drop repeated colors within each row, then pack left” fit both visible demonstrations from version 2.0.0 but disagreed on the held-out query. Focused RED tests also exposed ordinary samples on which ascending sort, descending sort, row reversal, or duplicate dropping produced the same answer as the intended order-preserving rule.
+
+Version 2.1.0 now constructs one compact teaching row in every ordinary sample. Four nonzero cells at separated columns carry `[mid, low, mid, high]` for three distinct sampled colors. This row preserves the canonical domain while simultaneously supplying a repeat, a descent, an ascent, and a non-palindromic order. Therefore sorting either direction, reversing, and deduplicating all disagree with the canonical packed row in each individual example; other rows remain randomized as before.
+
+`workspace/audit_learnability.py` is a standalone reusable finite-rival audit. Across 256 seeds, easy/medium/hard, two demonstrations, and one held-out query (768 episodes), none of the four declared rivals survived. Five focused tests plus 768 subtests pass, including clean-environment CLI execution. Protected Racket reproduced both pre-repair demonstrations, the disagreeing query, and the repaired sample exactly. The clean result excludes only those finite rivals over the exercised window; it is not a universal uniqueness proof.
